{"attempt_id": "attempt:um-kou-21.34-kourovka-notebook-problem-21.34-b5cb1acd-source-fidelity-01", "created_at": "2026-09-06T08:00:00Z", "graph_id": "graph:um-kou-21.34-kourovka-notebook-problem-21.34-b5cb1acd-v1", "obligations": [{"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof", "counterexample", "formalization", "computation"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": ["obligation:um-kou-21.34-kourovka-notebook-problem-21.34-b5cb1acd-statement-fidelity"], "kind": "root_claim", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-kou-21.34-kourovka-notebook-problem-21.34-b5cb1acd-root", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/KOU-21.34"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "(Well-known problem). A group $G$ is a unique product group if, for any nonempty finite subsets $A,B$ of $G$, there exists an element of $G$ which can be written uniquely as $ab$ with $a\\in A$ and $b\\in B$. A group $G$ is locally invariant orderable if $G$ admits a partial order $<$ such that for all $g,h\\in G$ with $h\\ne 1$, we have either $gh>g$ or $gh^{-1}>g$. Does there exist a unique product group which is not locally invariant orderable?"}, "statement_sha256": "732e66f115d3d72026106a92bcafa0bf64fc40b1b83f603ad0a855e8dd7bc574"}, {"acceptance": {"allowed_candidate_kinds": ["proof"], "required_capabilities": ["human_review", "statement_faithfulness"]}, "dependencies": [], "kind": "definition", "obligation_id": "obligation:um-kou-21.34-kourovka-notebook-problem-21.34-b5cb1acd-statement-fidelity", "source_refs": ["https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/KOU-21.34"], "statement": {"formal_declaration": null, "language": "en", "text": "Verify against the cited source that the frozen statement is complete, current, untruncated, attribution-correct, and unambiguous enough for mathematical research; record any definition or quantifier gap without silently editing the contract."}, "statement_sha256": "65f0b3d574bd6d837403149